/**
 * ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 *  GENE KEYS — ATTRACTION  (position-specific readings)
 * ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 *  The prenatal Moon: the magnetism one. Who your life pulls toward you and when — the field around you that draws certain people in at certain times, without you doing anything about it.
 *
 *  Mastery empowers: it names the capacity as an earned strength with the
 *  reader as subject and actor of nearly every sentence, surfaces something
 *  they have underrated in themselves, and ends on a lift rather than a
 *  caveat.
 *
 *  Written from the published Shadow/Gift/Siddhi triads and this sphere's
 *  lens alone — never from the universal reading or a sibling sphere, which
 *  is what stops the readings collapsing into rewordings of one another.
 *  Verified for shape, for distinctness against the universal reading and
 *  every shipped sphere, and for cross-sphere opening clauses.
 * ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 */

DGKRoles.register('attraction', {
  "1": {
    "gift": "You draw people who have reached the end of something and cannot yet see what replaces it, and they find you without being introduced. They arrive worn down and slightly ashamed of it, and the field around you hands them back the idea that beginning again is ordinary. Most people quietly repel the depleted, which is why the depleted travel so far to sit near someone who does not; you have never counted this as anything. What arrives at your door is rarely tidy, and it leaves your company visibly lighter, which is the plainest evidence you have that the pull is real.",
    "shadow": "Left unattended, the same field starts collecting people who want your freshness as a substitute for their own, and they settle in rather than move on. The trigger is usually your own flatness — a stretch where you stop renewing anything, and the ones who arrive then are simply reflections of that stall. Underneath sits a belief that you are only interesting mid-change, which makes ordinary continuity feel like a slow death and makes you tolerate company you would never choose rested. The cost is a circle of people who all met you at the same low point and now expect you to stay there with them.",
    "invitation": "What gathers around a field like this no longer needs freshness supplied to it, because the field itself has stopped going stale. Beauty simply arrives through the space between people the moment nothing there is being defended. Nobody is drawn by a performance anymore; they are drawn by weather that has nowhere left to hide."
  },
  "2": {
    "gift": "You gather people who have lost their bearings, and they arrive with the specific look of someone who no longer knows which way they were facing. They come near you and something settles, and their direction returns before you have offered a single piece of advice about their situation. Most people transmit their own uncertainty into a room, which is why yours feels so unusual to anyone who has been drifting for months. You have treated this as nothing more than being calm, when what actually travels from you is a working sense of where the centre is.",
    "shadow": "The pull turns costly when the disoriented start arriving faster than they leave, because being the fixed point is a role people take up permanently once offered. Answer the question of where to go before they have sat with being lost, and you inherit a following of people who will not navigate for themselves. Underneath runs an old fear of being adrift yourself, which is why steering others feels safer than admitting you are between chapters too. Then the field inverts: you attract the confidently wrong, people whose certainty you borrow, and you can spend the better part of a year facing a direction that was never yours at all.",
    "invitation": "The field stops sorting the lost from the found, because there is no longer a fixed point separate from what arrives near it. Unity means direction and destination have collapsed into the same single motion. What gathers here simply continues what was already whole, the way everything eventually finds the same river."
  },
  "3": {
    "gift": "You disarm people faster than you realise, and those who reach you arrive mid-mess, holding a situation with no settled shape to it yet. They come because you do not flinch at unfinished things, and around you the half-built version of their life stops being embarrassing. Where most company presses for a plan, yours presses for nothing, and that difference is why people bring you the raw material rather than the summary. You count this as simple curiosity, though very few adults have kept the ability to look at a mess without immediately trying to tidy it.",
    "shadow": "Trouble starts when the mess itself becomes the attraction, and you find your circle populated entirely by people whose lives are permanently on fire. The trigger is boredom — a quiet week reads as a dead one, so you lean toward whoever is generating the most noise and call it aliveness. Beneath that sits a suspicion that you are only wanted in an emergency, which makes steady people feel like a rejection rather than a rest. The cost is measurable: friendships that only activate under crisis, and a slow loss of anyone who wanted the ordinary version of you. Notice who stopped calling during the calm stretches, because that list is the honest measure of what your field has been rewarding.",
    "invitation": "Innocence is a field with nothing left to protect from what walks into it. Every arrival is met exactly as it is, with no story yet built about what it means or what it costs. This is a field that keeps nothing, and so has nothing left to lose."
  },
  "4": {
    "gift": "You receive people carrying a question about themselves that nobody in their life has been willing to sit inside without answering it for them. They come because your attention withholds the verdict, and a person who expects judgement and meets patience will tell you things they have never said aloud. Most listeners are assembling a response while someone speaks; you are assembling the person, which is a slower and far rarer thing to do. You assume everyone hears the reason behind a badly explained story, and they do not, and that gap is where your pull actually lives.",
    "shadow": "The same opening draws people who want a ruling rather than a hearing, and they keep coming back until you supply one. It gets worse under pressure: when your own tolerance is thin, the field flips and starts pulling in the people you find hardest, the certain, the loud, the ones who never wonder. What sits underneath is a belief that understanding someone obliges you to accept everything about them, so you either over-open or shut entirely, with nothing in between. The cost lands in your closest relationships, where years of listening produce a quiet resentment nobody sees coming, least of all the person who thought you were fine with it.",
    "invitation": "Forgiveness is the atmosphere itself now, not a stance taken toward whoever comes close. Every old account anyone might have opened has already been closed before the meeting began. What arrives here finds nothing being held against it, and carries nothing away that needed absolving."
  },
  "5": {
    "gift": "You pull toward you the people who are running out of road, the ones working to a deadline they did not set and cannot meet. They arrive at speed and slow within minutes, because your relationship with time was never borrowed from theirs and does not bend to their panic. Most company amplifies urgency without meaning to; yours absorbs it, which is why people in a hurry seek you out over better-qualified friends. You have filed this under being unbothered, when what you actually carry is a different clock, and people feel the difference the moment they sit down.",
    "shadow": "Held too long, the waiting becomes a policy, and the field fills instead with people chasing company in their stalling rather than movement. What sets it off is a decision you are dodging: patience turns into a hiding place, and whoever turns up in that stretch is fellow avoiders who flatter it. Underneath runs a fear that acting at the wrong moment costs everything, which guarantees that no moment ever qualifies as the right one. The opposite failure shows up in bursts, where stored impatience lands on people who were keeping your pace in good faith and did nothing wrong. Both versions cost the same thing — relationships that never reach the part where something actually happens.",
    "invitation": "Timelessness means the field has stopped keeping anyone's schedule, including its own. Whoever arrives, arrives exactly on time, because there was never actually a different time they could have come. What remains is a field that was never in any hurry to be reached."
  },
  "6": {
    "gift": "You find yourself standing between people who disagree, and this has happened so consistently that you stopped calling it coincidence a long time ago. Both sides approach you separately, each privately certain that you lean their way, and neither of them is entirely wrong about what they felt. Where most people choose a side inside the first minute, you hold two positions at once without strain, and opponents sense it before testing it. You underrate this badly, because the fights that never happened around you leave no trace behind and earn you no thanks from anyone involved.",
    "shadow": "The pull toward other people’s friction does not switch off when you are depleted, and a tired version of this draws the fight instead of the settlement. Being asked to take a side sets it going, at which point you either vanish entirely or commit hard to whoever pressed you most recently. What sits underneath is an old conviction that open disagreement ends relationships, so you smooth things that needed to stay rough for another week. Then the field fills with people who enjoy conflict itself and use your presence as an audience, which leaves you exhausted by arguments that were never yours. The cost is that your own disagreements go permanently unspoken, and the people closest to you never learn where your actual edges are.",
    "invitation": "Peace is what is left in a field once the need to smooth anything over has fully burned off. Disagreement can pass straight through it without leaving a mark on anyone who came close. Nothing here is being reconciled, because nothing here experiences itself as divided."
  },
  "7": {
    "gift": "You acquire people who want to be led, and they attach quietly, long before anybody announces that you are guiding anything at all. They arrive at forks in their own lives, and what they want from you is not instruction but the sight of someone walking their own line. Most people who end up with followers went out and found them; yours turn up unrecruited, which is a genuinely different phenomenon and worth naming. You treat your standards as private housekeeping, when in fact they are the visible thing, and people orient to them from a surprising distance.",
    "shadow": "The trouble arrives when the gathering becomes the point, and you start collecting people who agree with you rather than people who are going somewhere. It is triggered by doubt: an unsteady week makes an audience feel like proof, and the ones who show up then are those who never question anything. Beneath it runs a belief that you are only worth following while you are certain, which quietly bans you from saying you do not know. The other failure is division — taking a position so hard that your circle splits, and half the people who trusted you become opposition overnight. Both leave you surrounded by loyalty rather than company, and loyalty is a poor substitute at eleven at night.",
    "invitation": "Virtue is guidance moving through a field with no one left standing at its centre wanting to be followed. Whoever draws near takes what they came for and continues on, often without realising anything was given. This is influence that has stopped needing anyone to notice it."
  },
  "8": {
    "gift": "You collect people who are tired of the generic version of everything and have been looking for something with an actual flavour to it. They notice you before they have language for it, and what they respond to is specificity — the particular way you do a small ordinary thing. Most people broaden themselves to be liked by more; you narrowed, and the narrowing is exactly what made the pull selective and strong. You assume everyone has a distinct signature and simply hides it, which is not true, and the people who arrive are proof of the difference.",
    "shadow": "The pull sours when it starts drawing admirers of the surface, people who want the look and have no interest in the person underneath it. Comparison sets it off: a stretch of feeling ordinary sends you reaching for effect, and effect attracts an audience rather than company. Under it sits the fear that without the distinctive edge you are entirely unremarkable, which makes every plain day feel like evidence against you. There is a quieter failure where you dismiss anyone who does not meet your standard, and the field thins until only imitators remain. The cost is intimacy: people copy what you show and never get near what you are, and the loneliness of that is specific and real.",
    "invitation": "Exquisiteness is a field with every excess gesture already gone from it. What remains has stopped distinguishing itself from anyone in order to be found; it is simply itself, undecorated, and that is what people arrive for now. Nothing here is trying to be found interesting."
  },
  "9": {
    "gift": "You keep meeting people who have almost stopped — a project, a marriage, a plan sitting one inch from the finish and refusing to move. They come toward you at exactly that point, because something in your company makes the remaining distance look survivable rather than completely absurd. Most people offer encouragement, which evaporates; you offer the plain fact that small movement continues to work, and that lands differently on someone stalled. You call this stubbornness and think little of it, though the number of adults who finish anything difficult is far smaller than you imagine.",
    "shadow": "The heaviness comes when the stalled start arriving faster than they move, and your steadiness becomes the engine for lives that are not yours. Your own inertia sets it off: a period of not moving makes you far more available to other people’s stuckness, which feels like usefulness. There is a belief underneath that stopping means failing, so rest never happens and neither does the honest conversation about what is worth abandoning. The field then fills with borrowers — people who take a push, disappear, and return the moment they stall again, giving nothing back between visits. What it costs is the difference between being relied on and being known, and the second one quietly disappears without any announcement.",
    "invitation": "Invincibility is a field that no longer separates the person who stays from the person who stalled. Everyone who arrives is already met completely, with nothing held in reserve for someone more deserving later. Nothing can interrupt this field, because it isn't waiting on anyone in particular."
  },
  "10": {
    "gift": "You relax the people who come near you, and they drop their performance within a few minutes of sitting down, without noticing that they did it. The ones who find you are tired of managing an impression somewhere else, and your company is where that management stops being necessary. Most people require a version of you before they settle; the arrivals here get the unedited one immediately, and that is why they keep returning. You think of this as having no particular manner, when in fact the absence of a manner is the rarest thing in most rooms.",
    "shadow": "The reversal is unmistakable: when self-consciousness takes hold, the field stops relaxing people and starts recruiting an audience for your own running commentary. Being observed by someone whose opinion matters sets it going, after which every gesture gets checked and the people around you go quiet. What drives it is a fear that unmanaged, you are not much to look at, so the management goes on and the naturalness goes off. Then the arrivals change character — self-absorbed people who need a listener and mirror your own preoccupation back at you without ever asking a question. The cost is subtle and expensive: nobody relaxes near you anymore, and you lose the one thing you were unusually good at providing.",
    "invitation": "Being is a field that requires no performance from anyone standing in it, including the one standing at its centre. What gathers here simply gathers, the way people gather near a fire without anyone announcing that warmth is available. Naturalness has become the whole of what draws anyone closer."
  },
  "11": {
    "gift": "You host other people’s unspoken visions, and they hand them over early, before the idea has a name or any real chance of working. What draws them is that you take the unformed seriously, which almost nobody does, and a fragile idea survives its first hour in your company. Most listeners test an idea for feasibility immediately; you test it for what it is reaching toward, and dreamers know instantly which they are getting. You have assumed this is basic kindness, though the number of people who make room for an unproven hope is genuinely small.",
    "shadow": "It curdles when the hope becomes the whole diet, and the people arriving are those who prefer the vision to any version of it that could be built. Disappointment is what starts it: after something real falls through, the imagined future becomes safer, and you begin drawing fellow escapees who never land either. Below that runs a fear that the ordinary world has nothing in it for you, so the ordinary people quietly stop being invited. A second failure is fog — you speak in glow rather than specifics, and the ones who need clarity drift off while the vague ones move in. The cost shows up years later as a circle full of promise and short on anyone who has finished a single thing.",
    "invitation": "Light is a field that has stopped needing a vision worth joining, because everyone who arrives already carries it with them. Idealism is no longer a private picture offered to a room; it is simply how the room now looks from inside it. What gathers here is incidental to the light itself."
  },
  "12": {
    "gift": "You invite honesty out of people, and the ones who seek you out are looking for a true sentence about themselves from somebody. They come because your approval is not automatic, and a compliment from you carries weight precisely because it arrives so rarely and lands so specifically. Most company gives everyone the same warmth, which makes it worthless as information; yours discriminates, and people are hungrier for that than they admit. You have never counted your reluctance to gush as a gift, and yet it is the exact reason certain people keep coming back for a verdict.",
    "shadow": "The discernment turns brittle when it becomes a habit of rejection, and the field thins to people who either flatter you or fear you. Feeling unseen is what sharpens it: a stretch of not being valued raises the standard, and the raised standard drives away exactly the people who were genuine. Under that lies a belief that being liked broadly means being nothing in particular, so warmth gets withheld as a form of self-protection. The mirrored failure is vanity — attracting an audience for your taste rather than company for your life, and mistaking the two for a long time. What it costs is plain: people stop telling you the truth first, because they are busy passing your inspection instead.",
    "invitation": "Purity is a field with nothing left in it that was ever performed for whoever might be watching. What arrives is met without the question of how the meeting will be received, because that question has genuinely stopped arising. Nothing here is curated; everything here is simply what is true."
  },
  "13": {
    "gift": "You hear the sentence a person stops halfway through, and they know you did, which is why strangers tell you things their friends have never heard. The ones who arrive are carrying something undisclosed, and the disclosure happens on a bus, in a kitchen, at the end of a long day. Most people listen for their turn to speak; you listen for the shape underneath the words, and the difference is audible to whoever is talking. You have long assumed this is ordinary attention, and it is not — very few adults hear the second thing a person is saying.",
    "shadow": "The confessional pull has a cost nobody warns you about: you become a container, and containers do not get asked how they are. It is triggered by your own unspoken material, which makes another person’s disclosure both a relief and a distraction, so you invite more of it. Underneath sits a belief that you are welcome for what you absorb rather than for who you are, and so you never test it. The discord version is worse — hearing the undertone and reacting to it aloud, so people feel exposed and pull away without explaining why. The cost lands as a wide circle of people who trust you completely and a very short list of people who know anything about you.",
    "invitation": "Empathy is a field that has stopped needing to understand anyone from the outside, because the distance that made understanding necessary has already closed. Whoever draws near finds they were already included before they arrived. There is no longer a listener and a stranger, only the listening itself, open."
  },
  "14": {
    "gift": "You emit a sense of enough, and people who have been running on scarcity find their way toward it from a considerable distance. What arrives around you is not need exactly but relief — company that stops calculating for an evening because the atmosphere is not rationed. Most generous people give things; you give the impression that there is no shortage, which changes how the other person behaves before anything is handed over. You have never thought of this as anything but ordinary hospitality, and yet almost nobody produces that feeling in a room without spending money.",
    "shadow": "Abundance without a limit attracts extraction, and the ones who arrive learn quickly that the supply does not appear to run out. Fear of being seen as tight sets it off, so the offer goes out before you have checked what you actually have left. Beneath it runs a belief that generosity is the only reason anybody stays, which turns every gift into a quiet payment for company. The compromise version is different — accepting less than the arrangement was worth so that nobody feels awkward, and then resenting them for taking it. Both leave the same residue: a circle of people who are fond of your abundance and unfamiliar with your limits, because you never showed them any.",
    "invitation": "Bounteousness is a field that discovered it was never actually short of anything to offer. What is given here costs nothing to give, because the old fear of running out has quietly dissolved. Whoever arrives finds an abundance with no ledger attached to it at all."
  },
  "15": {
    "gift": "You attract people with no pattern to them, and the assortment around you would puzzle anyone trying to work out what the common thread is. The pull runs in seasons, going quiet for months and then bringing three unusual arrivals in a fortnight, which is how it has always behaved. Most people gather a type; you gather extremes, and the strange range of your company is not an accident of circumstance but the actual signature. You have blamed yourself for the quiet stretches, though the field was never designed to run continuously, and the return is reliable in its own way.",
    "shadow": "The dullness arrives whenever you try to be a consistent, presentable version of yourself, and the field flattens almost immediately in response. Comparison with people whose lives look steady is what starts it, making your unevenness feel like a fault and normality feel like the cure. What sits underneath is a fear that the quiet stretches mean it has gone for good, so you force company and get crowds rather than closeness. The other failure is spending the whole pull outward, gathering many people and knowing none of them, until a full room reads as loneliness. The cost is real: the peculiar people who were drawn to your peculiarity stop finding it, because you filed it down to be easier.",
    "invitation": "Florescence is a field in permanent, effortless bloom, with no season it is waiting to enter before it welcomes anyone. Every stage of whoever arrives, raw, unfinished, uncertain, is already received as flowering on its own terms. Nothing here is early, and nothing here is too late."
  },
  "16": {
    "gift": "You spark interest in people who arrived completely flat, and they leave your company caring about something they held no opinion on that same morning. Whoever seeks you out has come for permission to be openly enthusiastic, which most adult settings quietly and very efficiently discourage in everybody. Most people transmit competence; you transmit appetite, and appetite travels between people in a way that competence has never once managed for anybody. You dismiss your own excitement as slightly childish, and yet it is the single feature that makes people rearrange an entire evening to be near you.",
    "shadow": "Indifference is the cost of the same wiring, and it shows up as a sudden loss of interest that other people experience as abandonment. Mastery arriving is the trigger: the moment a thing becomes competent it goes dead, and whoever you shared it with is left holding the enthusiasm alone. Underneath runs a belief that your worth is the spark itself, so commitment feels like the end of being interesting rather than the beginning of depth. The field then fills with dabblers who match your pace, arrive loudly, and vanish at exactly the point where something would have been built. What it costs is continuity — a long list of people who once felt chosen by your attention and cannot say what happened.",
    "invitation": "Mastery is a field where the distance between beginner and master has quietly stopped applying to anyone who arrives. Whoever draws near is simply met correctly, the way water finds its own level without deciding to. This is welcome with nobody standing there measuring the welcome."
  },
  "17": {
    "gift": "You summon a particular visitor: the person standing in front of a decision whose consequences they cannot see more than a week ahead. They arrive because your read on how things unfold has been correct enough times that word travels, quietly, without you ever advertising it. Most advice describes the present; yours describes the shape of the thing in two years, and people notice which kind they have been given. You treat the long view as obvious, and it is not — the great majority of people cannot hold a horizon and a next step simultaneously.",
    "shadow": "The same faculty hardens into opinion, and then the people arriving are those who want their existing view confirmed by somebody who sounds certain. Being contradicted is the trigger: instead of looking again, you defend the position, and the wider the audience the harder the defending gets. Beneath that sits a fear that being wrong once erases every time you were right, which makes revision feel like a total loss of standing. A second pattern draws argumentative company — people who arrive for the sport of it, and you supply the sport every time without meaning to. The cost is that the ones with genuinely new information stop bringing it, because there is nowhere for it to land.",
    "invitation": "Omniscience is a field with no blind spot left in it, not because everyone in it has been studied, but because the need to have an opinion about anyone has stopped existing. Whoever arrives is already accounted for. Nothing here needed to be argued toward in advance."
  },
  "18": {
    "gift": "You register the flaw in an arrangement before anyone names it, and people who feel that something is wrong come toward you for confirmation. What they want is not criticism but accuracy — a clean description of the fault so the unease stops being a private suspicion. Most people either complain or say nothing; you locate the actual mechanism, which is why you are trusted with problems that other friends deflect. You have taken this for an irritable streak, and yet it functions as a repair instinct, and the people arriving know exactly what they came for.",
    "shadow": "The same eye becomes a habit of fault-finding, and then the company you keep is made of people who enjoy being critical together. Powerlessness is the trigger: unable to fix a situation, you describe its defects instead, and the description becomes the whole activity. Under it runs a belief that if you spot everything wrong first, nothing wrong will happen to you, which is an exhausting and unwinnable arrangement. There is an inward version too, where the standard turns on yourself, and the field empties because nobody feels adequate near that much scrutiny. The cost is that the people who improve things start avoiding you, and only the ones who like complaining stay in touch.",
    "invitation": "Perfection is a field with nothing left in it needing correction, because judgement itself has gone quiet. Whoever draws near is met exactly as they are, already precisely what the moment required. Nothing here can be improved, only witnessed."
  },
  "19": {
    "gift": "You read need in a room long before anybody voices it, and whoever happens to be carrying it arrives beside you without either of you arranging anything. They come because being noticed is rarer than being helped, and you notice first, which is the part they actually needed from somebody. Most people wait for a request; you register the absence of one, and lonely people recognise that capacity across a crowded room instantly. You have written this off as being sensitive, a word used against you, when it is closer to a form of accurate hearing.",
    "shadow": "The field turns heavy when the needy arrive faster than you set terms, and need is a very convincing imitation of closeness. What sets it off is an unmet want of your own, which you handle by attending to somebody else’s, and the arrangement holds until you are hollowed out. Underneath sits a belief that you are kept for what you provide, so asking for anything feels like putting the whole thing at risk. A second pattern is fusion — taking on another person’s state so completely that you lose the boundary and call the loss intimacy. The cost is a set of relationships where your own condition has never once been the subject, and nobody thinks to ask.",
    "invitation": "Sacrifice is a field that has stopped protecting its own needs from whoever arrives, not from depletion but because the separate self whose needs required protecting was never actually standing there. What is given costs nothing, because giver and arrival were never two things. This is welcome with no ledger left to balance."
  },
  "20": {
    "gift": "You anchor people into the current hour, and whoever comes toward you has been living several weeks ahead of themselves for a while. What they get is undivided attention with no agenda attached, which is scarce enough now that people will travel for twenty minutes of it. Most company is half-elsewhere; you are entirely in the room, and the difference registers physically in the other person before they articulate it. You have assumed everyone is capable of this and simply busy, and that assumption is wrong, which is why the pull is stronger than you think.",
    "shadow": "Superficiality is the cheap version, and it shows up as many brief contacts, each pleasant, none of them going anywhere at all. Discomfort with what a longer conversation would surface is the trigger, so you move on charmingly and call it having a wide life. Beneath it sits a fear that sustained attention would reveal there is not much there, which keeps every encounter conveniently short. The opposite failure is dominating the present moment so thoroughly that others become spectators, and spectators do not come back for a second evening. The cost arrives late: hundreds of warm acquaintances and almost nobody who knows the sequence of your last three years.",
    "invitation": "Presence is a field made entirely in the current instant, with no history behind it and no plan running ahead of whoever walks in. Whoever arrives, arrives into something already complete, the way a note arrives complete the moment it is struck. Nothing here is preparing for the next visitor."
  },
  "21": {
    "gift": "You inherit other people’s decisions, and it happens without appointment: a group hesitates, looks around, and settles on you as the one who will call it. The people who come toward you are carrying something unresolved that nobody else will take responsibility for, and they arrive relieved rather than resentful. Most people flinch at being the one held accountable; you do not, and that steadiness is visible from across a room to anyone who needs it. You have counted this as a burden dropped on you repeatedly, when in truth it is a response to something you broadcast constantly.",
    "shadow": "Control arrives when the authority stops being asked for and starts being taken, and the people who remain are those willing to be managed. A situation going sideways is the trigger: rather than tolerate the wobble, you take the wheel, and everyone else quietly stops developing any judgement. Below it runs a fear that unmanaged things fall apart and the falling apart will be attributed to you, which makes delegation feel reckless. The second failure attracts fighters — people who need to challenge whoever is in charge, so your closest relationships acquire an edge nobody wanted. The cost is that capable people go elsewhere, leaving you surrounded by the compliant and the combative, with nothing in between them.",
    "invitation": "Valour is authority with nothing left in the field that could be threatened by anyone who arrives. Whoever draws near meets a welcome already decided, cleanly, with the full weight of a life behind it. Command has dissolved into something closer to gravity, simply pulling what belongs."
  },
  "22": {
    "gift": "You take in the people who have just embarrassed themselves, and they find you at precisely the hour when everyone else has gone quiet. What draws them is that your manner does not change once they have fallen, and very few people manage that without a trace of pity. Most company treats disgrace as contagious; yours treats it as weather, and the person on the receiving end feels the difference immediately. You have thought of this as good manners, when in fact it is a form of steadiness that people remember for the rest of their lives.",
    "shadow": "Graciousness with no floor under it becomes a permission slip, and the ones arriving learn that behaviour has no consequence in your company. Your own dread of an ugly scene is the trigger, which makes you smooth over things that were asking for a real response. Underneath sits a belief that your worth depends on never being the cause of anyone’s discomfort, so the discomfort gets stored internally instead. The dishonour version turns inward — a private conviction that you are shameful, which makes you attract people who confirm it by treating you carelessly. The cost is a circle where your manners are appreciated and your standards are unknown, because you have never once shown anybody where they are.",
    "invitation": "Grace is a field with every last trace of self-consciousness gone from it. Whoever arrives is met the way weather arrives, without asking whether it is wanted, and welcomed regardless. Nothing here is trying to be gracious; it simply no longer knows another way to receive anyone."
  },
  "23": {
    "gift": "You meet people at the point where their situation has become too tangled to explain, and that is exactly when they think of you. They arrive with forty minutes of background and leave with one sentence, and the relief in the room is out of proportion to what you said. Most people add nuance to show they understood; you remove it, and removal is far harder and much less common than addition. You have treated this as impatience with detail, and yet what you are doing is finding the load-bearing part, which almost nobody does reliably.",
    "shadow": "The reduction turns violent when it arrives too early, and people feel their situation compressed rather than clarified, so they stop bringing you things. Your own overwhelm is what starts it: too many open threads, and the simplifying becomes a way of shutting things down rather than solving them. Underneath runs a belief that complication means you are not clever enough yet, which makes any messy problem quietly intolerable to sit with. The complexity version is the mirror — building elaborate structures around a simple decision so that nobody, including you, has to face it. The cost is that people bring you conclusions instead of the raw thing, and you end up solving a version that was already edited.",
    "invitation": "Quintessence is a field reduced to the one thing that was always underneath every other reason people might have had for coming close. Everything unnecessary about the welcome has already fallen away. What remains is the whole of it, in almost nothing."
  },
  "24": {
    "gift": "You interrupt the loop somebody has been going round for years, and the people who find you arrive deep into another repetition of it. What you offer is not willpower but a different route, and a new route is the only thing that has ever released a repeating pattern. Most people meet a stuck friend with encouragement; you meet them with an angle they had not considered, which is a rarer and more useful gift. You have never rated your habit of turning a problem sideways, and yet it is the specific reason people return to you after everything else failed.",
    "shadow": "The loop is not only other people’s, and the ones drawn in are those who become a habit for you rather than a relationship. A gap in the day starts it, unbearable in its blankness, filled by whoever answers quickly and reliably, whether or not they are good for you. Beneath it lies a belief that the quiet contains something you are avoiding, so any input is preferable to an empty evening. The other pattern is inventing endlessly and finishing nothing, so the field fills with people who love your ideas and never see one land. The cost is a life crowded with contact and short on the one thing that would settle it, which is an unfilled hour.",
    "invitation": "Silence is a field made from a mind with nothing left circling inside it, waiting to fill the space before anyone arrives. Whoever draws near is met whole, the way an answer arrives before the question finishes forming. This is welcome with the welcomer fully out of the way."
  },
  "25": {
    "gift": "You open a door that most people keep firmly shut, and the ones nobody else will take walk through it and sit down. They arrive carrying something they have been told is too much, and around you it stops being too much, which is the entire transaction. Most acceptance is conditional and everybody feels the conditions; yours has fewer of them, and that is detectable within the first five minutes. You have assumed this costs you nothing and therefore counts for nothing, and yet it is the reason certain people stayed when everybody else had gone.",
    "shadow": "The open door has no lock, and eventually the arrivals include people who want a permanent hospital rather than a passage through something. It is set off by your own untended hurt, which makes another person’s pain the easier one to sit beside, and so the ward fills up. Under it runs a belief that having a limit would make you like the people who shut the door on you, which is why no limit is ever set. Constriction is the other face — a hard closing after too much has come through, sudden and total, and the people cut off never understand what happened. The cost is a rhythm of total openness and total withdrawal, with nothing steady in between for anyone to rely on.",
    "invitation": "Universal Love is a field that no longer distinguishes who is permitted to come close. Every condition that once decided who belonged has quietly stopped mattering. What remains simply holds everyone, the way sky holds every kind of weather without preference."
  },
  "26": {
    "gift": "You slip into other people’s confidence quickly, and they hand you the thing they want said well to somebody who matters. The arrivals are people who cannot make their own case, and they know, correctly, that you will make it better than they would themselves. Most persuasive people are visible about it; your version leaves no fingerprints, which is why the trust extends further than it does for them. You have called this being good with words, and yet what you actually read is what the other side needs to hear, which is a separate skill.",
    "shadow": "Pride is what happens when the skill starts working for the reputation instead of the task, and the audience arrives to watch. Being underestimated is the trigger: a slight, real or imagined, and suddenly the case being made is your own worth rather than anything useful. Beneath sits a fear that without the performance nobody would pick you, which makes the ordinary, unimpressive version feel unsafe to show anyone. A different failure draws users — people who want your persuasion pointed at their situation and have no interest whatsoever in you personally. The cost is that admiration replaces affection, and admiration is a thin thing to be surrounded by when something goes badly wrong.",
    "invitation": "Invisibility is a field with no signature left on it, not from humility but because there is no longer anyone there needing the credit for having gathered anyone. Whoever arrives moves through unclaimed, more welcomed for there being no name attached to defend."
  },
  "27": {
    "gift": "You put food and shelter in front of people, and whoever is running on empty finds your address without anybody having given it to them. What arrives is a particular kind of guest: someone who has not been looked after in a long while and had stopped expecting it. Most care comes with an implied ledger; yours does not, and the absence of the ledger is what people register first and remember longest. You have assumed this is simply how households work, and it is not, and the number of people who count you as family is the evidence.",
    "shadow": "Care given without a limit trains the people around you to take it as a standing arrangement rather than a gift. Guilt is the trigger: the smallest suspicion of selfishness sends you back into providing, and the providing is what the arrangement runs on. Underneath runs a belief that the caring is the reason you are kept, so stopping feels less like rest and more like a test you would fail. The selfish version appears late and suddenly, as a withdrawal that shocks everybody, because the limit was never mentioned until it was reached. The cost is a household of people who love what you do and have never learned what you want.",
    "invitation": "Selflessness is a field with the line between host and guest fully collapsed. What is offered simply moves through, the way breath moves through a body, needing no accounting on either side. Nobody here was ever actually keeping anyone."
  },
  "28": {
    "gift": "You hold steady around loss in a way that draws people at the worst point of their lives toward your particular company. They come because you do not require them to be recovering yet, and almost every other well-meaning person quietly does require that. Most people talk about the dark part in the past tense; you are willing to be in it while it is still happening, which is the whole of the difference between you and them. You have taken your comfort with endings for a morbid streak, and it functions instead as a place where the frightened put things down.",
    "shadow": "Without a shape to it, the same appetite draws risk for its own sake and people who confuse intensity with meaning. A flat stretch is the trigger: nothing at stake, so you or somebody near you raises the stakes artificially, and the field fills with gamblers. Under that lies a fear that an ordinary life is a wasted one, which makes contentment feel like a slow disappearance rather than an arrival. The purposeless version is the opposite drift — no direction at all, drawing people who are also floating, and calling the shared drifting freedom. The cost is a circle organised around extremes, where nobody stays through the long, uneventful middle of anything.",
    "invitation": "Immortality is a field that has stopped needing anyone to stay in order to matter. Whoever arrives is already complete in the moment of arriving, with nothing riding on how long they remain. The old fear that time was running out has gone entirely quiet."
  },
  "29": {
    "gift": "You choose people completely, and the ones who find you have never been chosen quite like that by anybody else before. What they respond to is the absence of a reserve clause, since most affection arrives with an unspoken exit already drafted somewhere into it. Most people commit in stages and hedge the early ones; you commit once, whole, and the difference is felt long before it ever gets discussed. You have assumed this is just how people are with each other, and it is not, which is why your yes carries the weight it does.",
    "shadow": "Giving eighty percent is what goes wrong here, and it draws people who also keep one foot outside, so nothing between you ever quite starts. A previous commitment that cost you badly is the trigger, after which every new yes gets given at eighty percent and called realism. Underneath is the conviction that arriving whole means being swallowed, so a private reserve stays held back and the closeness never quite lands. The over-committed version is equally costly — saying yes to everything so that no single thing gets the whole of you, and everyone feels it. What it costs is the experience of being genuinely met, since the people who arrive are matching the level you are actually giving.",
    "invitation": "Devotion is a field given without measuring what comes back from it, not as a discipline but because the question of return has stopped arising at all. Whoever draws near is met completely, the way a candle gives its light without keeping any of it back."
  },
  "30": {
    "gift": "You light up whoever comes within range of you, and they leave wanting something again after a long stretch of wanting nothing much. The arrivals are people whose appetite has gone flat, and your company is where it comes back without anyone deciding to make that happen. Most enthusiasm demands to be matched; yours asks nothing, which is why people who are tired rather than dull are drawn to it so strongly. You have thought of your intensity as too much for most rooms, and yet the rooms keep filling, which is the more reliable piece of evidence.",
    "shadow": "Desire without lightness burns through people, and the field starts attracting those who arrive hot and disappear entirely within a season. Any dull afternoon is the trigger: the flatness feels intolerable, so you reach for a new person or a new fire and call it living. Under it runs a belief that the wanting itself is the aliveness, which makes having something feel like the beginning of its decay. The reverse failure is suppression, where the whole appetite gets clamped down, and the field goes cold along with it, drawing nobody at all. The cost is a history of consuming encounters and very few people who were there for both the heat and the ordinary Tuesday afterwards.",
    "invitation": "Rapture is every want this field ever generated, resolved into a welcome that already has what it was reaching for. Nothing here is trying to draw anyone in anymore, because the wanting itself has burned down to its own source. This is fulfilment with nothing left to chase."
  },
  "31": {
    "gift": "You call people together without ever issuing an invitation, and they gather because something in your voice suggests a direction that is worth following. The ones who arrive are looking for somebody to say the thing they have been thinking and could not put into words themselves. Most people speak to be agreed with; you speak to be checked, and an audience senses that distinction long before it has words for it. You have underrated how much it costs other people to say an unpopular sentence out loud, and how rare doing it plainly actually is.",
    "shadow": "Arrogance is not loudness here — it is speaking past the room, and the room notices before the speaker does. Doubt about whether the position is actually yours is the trigger, which makes the voice harden, and hardness attracts followers rather than colleagues. Beneath it lies a belief that authority survives only while you sound sure, so questions get treated as attacks and the good ones stop coming. The opposite pattern is silence: withholding the thing you know because saying it feels presumptuous, which leaves the field to whoever is loudest. The cost is a group that agrees with you publicly and organises quietly around somebody else, and nobody explains why to your face.",
    "invitation": "Humility is a field with the host fully dissolved back into whoever is gathered there. Influence still moves through it, but with no one standing at the centre collecting the credit for having drawn anyone close. What remains is welcome with nobody inside it wanting to be recognised as its source."
  },
  "32": {
    "gift": "You guard what other people are about to lose, and they sense it and bring the thing to you before they can explain why. The arrivals are custodians and heirs — anyone holding a business, a house, a story, or a practice that will outlive the person holding it. Most people are interested in what comes next; you are interested in what survives, and that orientation is uncommon enough to be felt immediately. You have treated your instinct for what lasts as caution, when in fact it functions as a memory the people around you rely on.",
    "shadow": "Preservation without judgement keeps the wrong things alive, and the people who arrive are those who want permission to hold on indefinitely. A loss you never processed is the trigger, which makes every ending look like the same ending, so nothing is allowed to finish. Underneath sits a fear of failing the people who came before, which turns inherited obligations into instructions you never agreed to follow. There is another version where anticipated failure keeps you from starting, and the company you keep becomes people who also never began anything. The cost is a life organised around what came before, with the people in front of you receiving whatever attention is left over.",
    "invitation": "Veneration is a field that reveres whoever arrives without needing an object separate from itself to revere. What draws close simply stays held, the way roots hold soil without being asked. Nothing here fears losing anyone, because nothing here is measured against keeping them."
  },
  "33": {
    "gift": "You remember people the way they hoped somebody one day would, and they feel that within the first few minutes of sitting down with you. The person who keeps arriving in your life is carrying a story they have told badly for years, and what they want is for it to be held whole this time. Nearly everyone listens for their own turn to speak, while you follow the thread the other person has lost, which is far less common than you assume it is. What gathers around you is not an audience but a handful of people who disappear for years, reappear without warning, and resume mid-sentence as though no time had passed.",
    "shadow": "Go absent from your own life and the arrivals change character entirely — pleasant people who need nothing remembered, gone again by spring. Vagueness reads as mystery to a stranger, so the pull is strongest exactly when you have least to give, and the wrong sort turn up in numbers. Underneath all of it sits a quiet conviction that anyone who recalled the whole of you, the earlier chapters included, would find a reason to leave. New faces meet the surface only, and then you wonder why nobody has stayed past a first difficult winter. What that costs is a crowded life with no witness inside it, and a slow suspicion that being properly known was never actually on offer.",
    "invitation": "Revelation is a field that no longer needs anyone's history explained to it before receiving them, because everything that mattered about them is already, continuously, present. Whoever arrives is not analysed, only finally seen. Nothing here was ever actually hidden from the field."
  },
  "34": {
    "gift": "You command attention without raising your voice, and a room orients toward you well before you have said anything worth hearing. The people who arrive have mostly been pushed around by somebody, and they want to stand near a person that pressure does not bend or hurry. Where others need visible agreement in the room to feel solid, you stay solid without it, and everybody present reads that instantly and finds it restful. The steadiness you have never thought twice about is the exact quality that keeps a certain kind of person circling back to you across many years.",
    "shadow": "Push instead of stand, and the field around you changes overnight into something much cruder. Raw force pulls in two types of company: people spoiling for a fight, and people wanting a leader so they never have to decide anything themselves again. Both are exhausting, and neither one actually sees you — one is looking at an opponent, the other at a wall to lean against. The driving idea is that softening even slightly would make everybody stop coming, when softening is precisely what filters the wrong ones out. Meanwhile the person capable of standing beside you as an equal takes one look at all that noise and keeps on walking.",
    "invitation": "Majesty is a field with nothing left to prove to whoever draws near it. What gathers here meets the full weight of a life, offered without display, the way a mountain doesn't announce its own size to whoever climbs it. This is a pull that has stopped needing an audience to confirm it."
  },
  "35": {
    "gift": "You promise motion, and people who have gone stale in their own lives sense it on you from right across a crowded room. Whoever seeks you out is at the end of something and looking for proof that a life changes direction without the whole structure falling apart. Others make a stuck person feel judged for being stuck, whereas you leave them with the impression that the door was never really locked. The people who last longest around you are the few who match your pace instead of asking you to slow down for their comfort.",
    "shadow": "Restlessness gathers a very particular crowd: people in the middle of a crisis who mistake your appetite for a rescue plan with their name on it. They arrive fast, take the momentum, and vanish once their own life restarts, which leaves you puzzling over why nothing survives past six months. Boredom is the trigger — the moment a connection settles into something ordinary, your attention slides toward whoever has just walked in. Buried in that is a fear that staying put would kill off the interesting part of you. The bill comes as the friendship that only reveals itself in year four, and a three-in-the-morning sense that everyone you know, you met recently.",
    "invitation": "Boundlessness is a field that has finally consumed its own edges. Nothing further is being chased toward the next arrival, because the sense of a next thing worth reaching for has quietly dissolved. What gathers here simply continues, complete in every direction at once."
  },
  "36": {
    "gift": "You absorb the unsayable without flinching, and a stranger registers it within a minute, before they have finished their opening sentence. Whoever finds you is in the middle of something unsayable at work and unsayable at home, and they say it to you anyway, within an hour of meeting. Most people flinch, change the subject, or reach for advice, while you simply stay in the room with the feeling, which is much less ordinary than you believe. Because of that, people recall exactly where they were sitting when they told you, years after the crisis itself has gone quiet.",
    "shadow": "Turbulence gathers turbulence, and when your own life is unsettled the arrivals get dramatic in a way that eats whole years. People living in permanent emergency find you unerringly, because the field around you announces that nothing said here will be shocking. They come for the steadiness, take as much as they need, and leave long before the exchange has ever run the other way. The false idea holding it together is that being needed at that intensity is the same thing as being loved, so nobody ever asks why the phone stays silent when things are fine. Left alone, it costs you the calm friendship, the ordinary Tuesday, and a nervous system that has forgotten peace.",
    "invitation": "Compassion is a field built entirely from what turbulence once taught it, with none of the original wound left to manage. Whoever arrives finds a welcome that already knows what crisis feels like from the inside, and holds it without flinching. This is a field with nothing left needing to be defended."
  },
  "37": {
    "gift": "You soften a room simply by walking into it, and people put their defences down near you without ever making that decision. Whoever comes toward you is hunting for somewhere they are permitted to stop performing competence, and such a place is visible from a considerable distance. Most people hand out warmth as a transaction with terms attached, while yours arrives plainly, and the difference registers on a newcomer inside an hour. Families, teams and whole friendship groups quietly rearrange themselves around you, which you rarely notice, because none of it is ever announced out loud.",
    "shadow": "Warmth handed out from need pulls in people who receive it as a service and never once think to send any back. Loneliness is the trigger — unsure of your place, you start feeding everybody, and those who show up are hungry rather than fond of you. Agreements get made that nobody said aloud, and years later you are still keeping them and quietly resenting every one. Beneath that lives a fear that the warmth is the sole reason anyone stays, which makes withdrawing it feel like gambling the entire relationship. The outcome is a circle of dependants where equals belong, and a tenderness that has hardened into an obligation nobody remembers agreeing to.",
    "invitation": "Who accepts your warmth without ever offering any of their own? Decline one small act of care this week and watch closely. You are allowed to be loved for something other than your usefulness. What shifts in the people around you when the warmth becomes a gift again rather than a duty?"
  },
  "38": {
    "gift": "You rally people who were about to give up, and they seem to appear at the precise point where their own reserves ran out. Whoever arrives wants evidence that persistence is possible without anybody pretending the situation is easy, and that is what you have offered since childhood. Most people encourage from a safe distance and go home, while you stand inside the difficulty alongside someone, and the difference is felt immediately. The loyalty this earns you is unusually hard-wearing, because it was formed during a bad stretch rather than a pleasant one, and that kind rarely breaks.",
    "shadow": "Struggle pursued for its own sake pulls in fights that nobody ever asked you to have. With no worthy cause in front of you, an unworthy one arrives on schedule, and you find yourself defending a person who was never going to change. Those who gather then are people needing a permanent champion, because their situation has been arranged so that it never resolves. Sitting under all of it is the notion that being needed for the fight is the only dependable way to be kept. The price is whole years spent inside other people's battles, while the friends who wanted your company rather than your armour drift off without explaining themselves.",
    "invitation": "Whose fight are you currently carrying that was never yours to carry? Put one battle down this week and stay exactly where you are. You are allowed to matter in peacetime as much as in war. If nobody at all needed defending, who would still choose to sit down beside you?"
  },
  "39": {
    "gift": "You provoke movement in people who have been stationary for years, and they feel it the instant a conversation with you turns real. Whoever seeks you out is quietly aware of having gone numb, and they want somebody willing to say the thing everyone else has agreed to leave unsaid. Most people smooth a conversation over to keep the table comfortable, while you press the sore spot gently, and something in the other person wakes up. What returns to you, months afterwards, is thanks from people whose lives altered course after a single evening spent with you.",
    "shadow": "Provocation with no aim behind it collects people who enjoy the friction and have no intention of changing a thing. Stagnation in your own life is the trigger — with nothing moving, you poke at whoever is nearest and give it the name honesty. The ones who remain are those who like sparks; anybody who could have loved you calmly is gone inside a month. Running underneath is a fear that if the stirring stopped the room would go quiet and nobody would notice you were in it. That costs you a social life built from intense, short, combustible attachments and almost nothing that outlives the flare itself.",
    "invitation": "Whom have you pushed away with one sentence sharper than it needed to be? Let a conversation stay peaceful this week when instinct says otherwise. You are allowed to be interesting without disturbing anything at all. When the friction drops away, what kind of person finally has room to come close?"
  },
  "40": {
    "gift": "You refuse things that most people accept without question, and that refusal is visible to a stranger before you have explained a word of it. The company you draw is made of people who have never once said no in their lives and are quietly studying how it is done. Others give until they are hollow and call the result generosity, whereas you give hard and then close the door, which onlookers find shocking and secretly instructive. Every relationship that lasts with you has survived a boundary at some point, and that makes the whole set of them sturdier than most people ever manage.",
    "shadow": "Exhaustion rewrites the field completely, and what turns up is everybody who has ever wanted something from anyone. Depleted, the refusals stop, and the takers sense from a long way off that the gate is standing open. Guilt is the trigger: one sentence about how badly they are struggling and the no you had ready dissolves before it reaches your mouth. There is a false idea in there that the refusal would end the relationship, when in truth the relationships worth keeping are precisely the ones that survive it. The damage lands physically first and socially second — a circle full of people you cannot decline, and no way left to rest.",
    "invitation": "Who is in your life right now purely because you never refused them? Say one clean no this week and leave it entirely unsoftened. You are allowed to keep only the people who can hear that word. What sort of company arrives once your gate has a working latch on it?"
  },
  "41": {
    "gift": "You plant things in people that surface much later, and they date the whole change back to one conversation with you. Whoever arrives is at the very start of something fragile, and they want the company of a person who takes a beginning seriously instead of asking for the plan. Most people require a finished proposal before they will get excited, while you feel the shape of a thing before it exists, and others experience that as permission. Your closest connections are with people who met you at the hinge point of their lives, which is why none of them ever quite let you go.",
    "shadow": "Fantasy pulls in people who are in love with the version of you that lives entirely in the future. They arrive for the vision, stay while it is being described, and lose interest the moment ordinary life walks in and sits down. Disappointment with the present is the trigger — when now feels flat you begin narrating what might be, and the crowd that gathers is full of dreamers with no follow-through. Beneath that runs an assumption that the real you only becomes lovable once the imagined life has finally arrived. The cost is the person standing directly in front of you, who wanted your ordinary Wednesday and was never once offered it.",
    "invitation": "Who has waited a long time for the version of you that keeps being postponed? Describe your actual life to somebody this week without improving it anywhere. You are allowed to be met exactly where you already stand. What arrives in your life once you stop introducing yourself as a plan?"
  },
  "42": {
    "gift": "You release a grip nobody else notices, and the person freed by it comes back toward you for the rest of their life. The ones drawn in are carrying something they have no idea how to set down, and they want to sit near somebody who has clearly set something down before. Most people cling quietly and give it the name loyalty, while you let a relationship end without turning the ending into a wound, which is rarer than you think. Because you never grip, everyone still around you actively chose to be there, and the difference is something you feel daily.",
    "shadow": "Detachment worn as armour attracts people who are relieved to meet someone with no expectations of them whatsoever. They come because nothing is asked, stay lightly, and leave without ceremony, which then confirms the old story that nothing ever lasts. Anticipated loss is the trigger — sensing an ending, you let go months early, and the ending duly arrives right on schedule. There is a false conviction in there that wanting something out loud is what causes it to be taken away from you. The result is a lifetime of graceful goodbyes and very few arrivals, plus a numbness that looks like wisdom from outside and feels like absence from within.",
    "invitation": "Which person did you let go of well before they had actually left? Tell one person plainly that you would like them around. You are allowed to hold on to what you have not lost. If you stopped rehearsing the ending, what kind of person might risk staying to the finish?"
  },
  "43": {
    "gift": "You puzzle people initially, and one particular sort of person finds that far more compelling than being understood at first glance. The ones who come your way are tired of obvious company and want a mind that gets to its conclusions sideways rather than in a straight line. Most people require quick understanding in order to feel safe, while you sit unexplained for months, which the right person reads as depth instead of distance. The friendships you gather are slow to form and near impossible to break once somebody has taken the trouble to learn your language.",
    "shadow": "Going unheard for long enough curdles into not bothering, and then the people who appear are the ones who never ask you anything. Isolation is the trigger — a stretch alone and your inner language drifts far enough that nobody follows, so only the incurious stay comfortable nearby. That leaves a room full of company who like your presence and have no real idea what you think about anything. Running under it is a conviction that explaining yourself has never worked before and will not work now either. The cost is a peculiar loneliness in company, and the loss of the one or two people who genuinely would have understood, given the chance.",
    "invitation": "Who last made a serious attempt to follow your thinking? Say one half-formed thought aloud this week before it is finished. You are allowed to be understood without translating everything first. When you let somebody in early, what kind of listener turns out to have been available all along?"
  },
  "44": {
    "gift": "You recognise people, occasionally before you have properly met them, and something in them recognises you straight back. Those arriving in your life are people you already share a history with, whether or not that shared history has technically taken place in this life yet. Most people assemble a network on purpose, while yours accumulates by instinct, since you know within minutes who belongs near you and who does not. The teams and circles that gather around you outlast every deliberate, organised attempt made by anybody else in the room, and you rarely take credit for that.",
    "shadow": "Old patterns summon old faces, and when you are unwell the same character shows up in a new body every few years. Interference is the trigger — meddling in who ought to know whom, arranging people like furniture, until the group becomes a tangle of obligations nobody enjoys. Those who gather then have unfinished business, drawn by the sense that you will happily replay the whole thing with them. Beneath it lives an idea that these repeats are fate rather than habit, which makes them almost impossible to interrupt. Decades go by circling the same handful of dynamics, while the genuinely new person walks past entirely unrecognised.",
    "invitation": "Which face keeps returning to your life wearing a different name each time? Notice who arrives next, before you decide which old face they resemble. You are allowed to meet somebody entirely new. What becomes possible when the pattern stops choosing your company on your behalf?"
  },
  "45": {
    "gift": "You unite the mismatched, and they gather around you without anybody having posted an invitation or drawn up a guest list. Whoever shows up is hunting for somewhere to belong that asks them to leave nothing of themselves at the door, not even the awkward part. Most hosts hold a group together by setting terms, while you hold one together by widening the table, and a newcomer feels that difference within an evening. The circle around you ends up unusually mixed — three or four people who share nothing but you — which is the reason it outlasts tighter, tidier groups by years.",
    "shadow": "Dominance alters the shape of the gathering, and those who keep coming are the ones content to be presided over. Insecurity about your standing is the trigger — unsure of it, you take the head of the table, and everybody with a spine of their own stops attending. What remains is a court rather than a community, agreeable and hollow, and the difference is obvious to you without ever being nameable. Sitting underneath is a fear that a group left to organise itself would not include you at all. You end up with a lifetime of gatherings that you arranged and never once enjoyed being inside.",
    "invitation": "Who stopped coming to your table and never gave a reason? Let somebody else set the agenda at the next gathering. You are allowed to belong somewhere without presiding over it. Where does real communion begin once you are one of the people at the table rather than its head?"
  },
  "46": {
    "gift": "You delight people almost accidentally, and they seek you out afterwards without being able to name what they came for. The ones drawn in have been taking life very seriously for a long stretch now and are quietly after permission to stop for one evening. Most people work hard at being good company, while you are good company by default, and that is a genuine rarity you consistently underrate in yourself. What comes back are invitations from people you barely know, simply because a room measurably improves whenever you happen to be standing somewhere inside it.",
    "shadow": "Seriousness settles over you and the pull goes quiet, which is disorienting, since attraction has never been something you had to think about. A stretch of grinding effort is the usual trigger — head down, pleasure postponed, and the invitations thin out without anybody consciously deciding anything. Those who do arrive want your competence rather than your company, and the exchange grows heavier by the month. Buried in this is the belief that lightness has to be earned by finishing everything else first. What it costs you is a calendar stuffed with obligation and empty of anybody who turned up simply because they enjoy you.",
    "invitation": "When did somebody last seek you out purely for the fun of it? Accept one invitation this week that serves no purpose whatsoever. You are allowed to be chosen for your lightness. What kind of person reappears in your life once delight is back on offer from you?"
  },
  "47": {
    "gift": "You carry difficult material well, and anyone hauling their own recognises it in you at a single glance across a crowded room. The people who find you are somewhere in the middle of a bad year, wanting the company of somebody who has been through one and came out without shattering. Most people offer sympathy from outside the experience, while you speak from inside it, and that difference is unmistakable to whoever is listening. Bonds formed in those particular conversations hold for decades, long after the circumstances that created them have disappeared entirely from both of your lives.",
    "shadow": "Sitting too long in the dark changes what the field draws in, and the arrivals want company in their own darkness rather than a way out of it. Oppression that has gone unspoken is the trigger — carried silently, it broadcasts, and everybody hauling a similar weight gravitates toward you. Nothing transmutes then; it simply pools, and you start to believe that heaviness is the only honest way to be with another person. Underneath sits a suspicion that lighter people would neither want you nor understand a word of it. Years pass without a single person in your life who is actually well, and the isolation goes unnoticed because it feels like depth.",
    "invitation": "Which friend of yours is genuinely doing fine at the moment? Spend an evening with somebody whose life is uncomplicated right now. You are allowed to be excellent company when nothing whatsoever is wrong. If the weight lifted tomorrow morning, who would you want sitting across the table from you?"
  },
  "48": {
    "gift": "You admit what you do not know, and people find that so unusual that they return specifically for another dose of it later. The ones drawn toward you are surrounded by certainty everywhere else and quietly starving for somebody honest about the limits of their own knowledge. Most people bluff to protect their position, while you say the uncertain thing out loud, and everybody in the room relaxes by a small but visible fraction. The trust you accumulate this way runs deeper than the trust experts get, because nothing you have ever said needed defending later on.",
    "shadow": "Feeling inadequate pulls in people who are glad to confirm it, and they arrive carrying advice that nobody requested. Comparison is the trigger — one glance at somebody more established and you shrink, and the shrinking is legible to exactly the wrong audience. Those who gather then need somebody smaller nearby in order to feel large themselves, and that arrangement runs for years before you spot it. A false conviction underpins the whole thing: that you have to be finished before you are worth anybody's time. The result is a circle of people who prefer you uncertain and will never once encourage you out of it.",
    "invitation": "Who benefits from you doubting yourself as much as you do? Say one thing this week that you know to be true. You are allowed to be treated as somebody with something to offer. Where does real wisdom find you once you stop apologising for the gaps in what you know?"
  },
  "49": {
    "gift": "You unsettle assumptions merely by being present, and a certain kind of person is magnetised by that within minutes of meeting you. The people arriving are at the end of an arrangement that no longer fits, and they need somebody entirely unafraid of an ending. Most people counsel patience when a life clearly needs changing, while you say the true thing early, which lands as either alarming or liberating with very little in between. Those who choose you afterwards wanted the truth more than they wanted the comfort, and that sort of person never really leaves.",
    "shadow": "Reactivity sets the tone, and in comes everybody who enjoys watching something get burned to the ground. A small slight is the trigger — one dismissal and the entire relationship is reassessed and terminated inside an afternoon. The company gathering then are people mid-revolt themselves, after an ally rather than a friend, and the alliance dissolves the moment their particular fire goes out. Sitting beneath it is the belief that leaving first is safer than being left behind. The damage is a trail of endings that were mostly unnecessary, plus a reputation that keeps the steadier and slower people at a careful, permanent distance.",
    "invitation": "Which relationship did you end inside a single afternoon? Wait three days before acting on your next surge of certainty. You are allowed to stay while something is still being repaired. When the endings slow down, what kind of person becomes willing to come closer to you?"
  },
  "50": {
    "gift": "You balance a group without appearing to do anything at all, and people gravitate toward that steadiness long before they could name it. Those arriving have come from somewhere disordered and want to sit near a person whose sense of what is right does not move around. Most people adjust their standards to suit the company present, while you hold yours quietly, and everyone in the room calibrates against it whether they mean to or not. The loyalty this earns is unusual in kind — people bring you their hardest decisions rather than their easiest ones, and they bring them early.",
    "shadow": "Let the standard slip and the field shifts fast, drawing in everybody who benefits from a rule bent slightly their way. Loyalty misapplied is the trigger — protecting a person who should not be protected, until everyone needing that sort of cover has found the route to your door. What builds is a circle held together by nothing but mutual compromise, comfortable and quietly rotten right through the middle. Beneath it runs a fear that holding the line would leave you standing entirely alone. The people who originally came for your integrity stop coming altogether, and they almost never tell you the reason they went.",
    "invitation": "Which person around you is protected by a standard you quietly lowered for them? Restore one line quietly this week without announcing that you have. You are allowed to keep company that survives your own standards. If the order held completely, who finds their way toward your door?"
  },
  "51": {
    "gift": "You wake people up, and they arrive in your life at the precise moment when they had become ready for it. Those who come have been asleep inside a comfortable arrangement, and they sense on meeting you that the arrangement is not going to continue much longer. Most people require years and a crisis to change direction, while you deliver the shock in one conversation, which others describe long afterwards as their turning point. What returns to you is a scattered set of people all over the world who count you as the reason something in them started moving.",
    "shadow": "Agitation with nowhere to discharge pulls in people who want a shock rather than an actual change. They come for the jolt, take it, and go hunting the next one, which leaves you feeling used in a way that is hard to put into words. Boredom with your own steadiness is the trigger — restless, you start something in somebody purely to feel the current move again. There is a fear in there that without the lightning nobody would come at all. It costs you every relationship requiring ordinary continuity, and builds a growing sense of being an event in people's lives rather than a presence in them.",
    "invitation": "Who currently treats you as a jolt rather than as a person? Keep in touch with one person right through an entirely uneventful month. You are allowed to matter to somebody quietly. What kind of awakening becomes possible once you are still there the following morning?"
  },
  "52": {
    "gift": "You still the noise other people carry in with them, and the fast-moving ones drift toward that without understanding what pulled them there. The company arriving is overstimulated and hunting for the one place where nothing whatsoever is being demanded of them at all. Most people fill a silence within seconds of it appearing, while you hold one comfortably, and the effect on an agitated person is immediate and physical. Those who stay near you longest are the ones who discovered they think more clearly in your company than they do anywhere else in their lives.",
    "shadow": "Stillness used as a hiding place looks identical from outside, and yet it draws a completely different crowd. Overload is the trigger — too much noise and you go entirely quiet, and the people approaching then are the ones who need no response from you at all. They talk, you absorb, and the exchange never once runs in both directions. Beneath that sits an assumption that staying unmoved will keep you from being disturbed, when mostly it keeps you from being known by anybody. Left running, it produces one-sided company and a stillness that stopped being peace some while ago without your ever noticing.",
    "invitation": "Who talks at you these days rather than with you? Break your own silence once this week, before anybody else thinks to ask. You are allowed to be still and present simultaneously. Where does real stillness lead when it is shared rather than used as cover?"
  },
  "53": {
    "gift": "You grow things by being near them, and people bring you their beginnings for precisely that reason and no other. Those arriving are holding something small and unformed that they have not yet dared show to anybody else in their life. Most people evaluate a new idea the instant they hear it, while you let it get bigger first, and the person opposite feels the difference inside a minute. People credit you with starts you barely remember taking part in, and that has happened far more times than you have ever bothered to sit down and count.",
    "shadow": "Immaturity in the field pulls in people who want beginnings and nothing beyond them, and they leave the moment anything requires maintenance. Impatience is usually the trigger — the first stage is thrilling, so you keep restarting, and the company you keep is permanently new. That builds a wide, shallow spread of acquaintances and almost nobody who has watched you through one complete cycle. Running under it is the belief that the interesting portion of any connection is its first month. The cost surfaces in middle age, when everyone else has people going back decades and yours all seem to have appeared last year.",
    "invitation": "Who has known you longest, and when did you last ring them? Return to one connection you abandoned in its early stage. You are allowed to be known by somebody across many years. If you let a friendship mature fully, what kind of abundance shows up that a beginning never could?"
  },
  "54": {
    "gift": "You climb in full view of everyone, and other climbers locate you almost immediately in any room you happen to walk into. Those coming toward you want the company of somebody genuinely willing to want more without apologising for wanting it. Most people conceal their ambition in order to seem agreeable, while you name yours, and that gives everyone else in the conversation permission to say theirs aloud. The alliances forming this way are candid in a rare way, because nothing whatsoever was concealed at the beginning, and candour of that kind is hard to find elsewhere.",
    "shadow": "Wanting out of scarcity changes who appears, and the people around you become useful rather than close. Every connection quietly acquires a purpose, and anyone who cannot serve one fades off the calendar without a decision being made anywhere. Comparison is always the trigger — somebody further ahead appears, and suddenly every relationship you have is being measured against that gap. Underneath runs the assumption that affection follows position, so nobody would remain if the position ever went. The bill arrives late: a full contact list, several people who owe you favours, and nobody at all who knew you before any of it began.",
    "invitation": "Whose presence in your life is useful to you and nothing else besides? Spend time with somebody who can do nothing for you. You are allowed to be wanted for who you already are. What kind of ascent becomes possible once the climbing has stopped being a lonely business?"
  },
  "55": {
    "gift": "You shine differently from one month to the next, and whoever comes toward you is drawn in by whichever version happens to be showing that week. The people who stay find the changes interesting rather than unreliable, and every one of them is worth a dozen of the others. Most people construct a consistent self in order to keep company, while you let yourself alter, which is a good deal braver than it looks from the outside. Over time you gather a small number of people who love the entire weather system instead of one favourite season.",
    "shadow": "Feeling wronged rearranges the field completely, and in come the collectors of unfairness, looking for somebody to compare wounds with. A genuine injustice that never got answered is the trigger, and the story about it starts introducing you before you have opened your mouth. Rescuers and fellow sufferers arrive, and both arrangements quietly require you to remain exactly as damaged as you currently are. Beneath this lives the belief that the hurt is the truest thing about you, and that releasing it would erase the record of what happened. The result is a circle organised entirely around your past, with no room left for whatever might happen next.",
    "invitation": "Who only knows the version of you that was wronged? Introduce yourself once this week without any of the history attached. You are allowed to be greeted as free rather than as damaged. When the old story stops arriving ahead of you, what kind of person walks toward you instead?"
  },
  "56": {
    "gift": "You wander, and the people gathered along the way are far more interesting than anything that a deliberate network could ever produce. Those coming toward you are after a wider world, and they see immediately that you have already spent time out in it. Most people collect their contacts inside a single field, while yours accumulate from everywhere, and that makes your own company genuinely unpredictable in a way others find refreshing. The stories you carry between those separate worlds are why so many people stay in touch with you across many years and several continents.",
    "shadow": "Distraction thins everything out, and the arrivals become people who are pleasant for one evening and never thought of again afterwards. Discomfort is the trigger — a difficult feeling shows up and you go hunting stimulation, and stimulation always comes with company attached to it. Such people are easy to find and impossible to keep, and the pattern repeats until your life is a long list of nearly-friends. Sitting underneath is the assumption that stopping still with any one person would let something unbearable catch up with you. It costs you depth, and eventually the recognition that nobody currently alive knows you particularly well.",
    "invitation": "Whom did you enjoy enormously and then never once contact again? Follow up this week with a person you liked and dropped. You are allowed to stay long enough to be properly known. If you stopped moving toward the next thing, who is already close enough for you to reach?"
  },
  "57": {
    "gift": "You sense who is about to arrive in your life, occasionally several days before they actually turn up. The people drawn toward you have something unspoken and feel that you already know it and are waiting politely for them to say it. Most people require being told before they understand a situation, while you pick it up out of the air in a room, which strikes others as uncanny and deeply reassuring. Those who stay closest never had to explain themselves to you twice, and that is far rarer company than you have ever once assumed.",
    "shadow": "Unease misread as intuition pulls in people who confirm the worry instead of settling it. A noisy environment or a rushed week is the trigger — the signal blurs, and you begin reading threat where none exists and warmth where there is nothing whatsoever. Whoever appears then feels familiar precisely because they resemble an old danger, and you mistake that recognition for genuine connection. Beneath it runs a belief that discomfort is always information and never simply tiredness catching up with you. The cost is a life spent braced, and the quiet, safe people who never register strongly enough for you to notice them at all.",
    "invitation": "Who felt wrong to you and turned out to be perfectly fine? Rest properly before forming any judgement about somebody new. You are allowed to trust a calm feeling as much as an alarming one. When the noise clears out, what kind of person becomes visible that you have been walking straight past?"
  },
  "58": {
    "gift": "You enliven people, and they notice their own energy climbing within about twenty minutes of sitting down opposite you. The people arriving are flat, overworked, or living very carefully, and something in your company reminds them that every bit of that was optional. Most people require a reason before they will feel good, while you generate it out of nothing, which you assume is perfectly normal and which very much is not. What returns to you are people quietly inventing excuses to be near you, mostly without ever realising that this is what they are doing.",
    "shadow": "Dissatisfaction has a pull of its own, and it gathers people who want to complain alongside you rather than change a single thing. One small wrong detail is the trigger — a single irritation and the whole evening reorganises around what is not right, with the company following suit. Fellow critics arrive, and the friendship runs entirely on shared grievance, which resembles closeness closely enough to fool both of you. Underneath sits the notion that spotting the flaw is a mark of intelligence rather than a habit picked up somewhere. The joyful people, the ones you actually want near you, gradually stop calling and nobody says why.",
    "invitation": "Whose company do you keep mainly for the complaining? Spend one whole conversation this week without naming a single problem. You are allowed to be enjoyable company rather than accurate company. Where does bliss begin once the people around you are no longer there to agree that things are bad?"
  },
  "59": {
    "gift": "You expose the real thing quickly, and people exhausted by pretending will cross an entire crowded room in order to reach you. Those arriving are heavily defended everywhere else in their lives and want one place where the armour comes off without comment. Most people take years to get past the pleasantries, while you manage the same thing inside a single evening, which others experience as relief rather than intrusion. The intimacy you draw forms fast and holds for a very long time, and it barely depends on how frequently the two of you actually see each other.",
    "shadow": "Small dishonesties alter the field faster than almost anything else, and what comes toward you are people running their own careful edits. Fear of being seen entirely is the trigger — one detail withheld to look better, and the connection settles permanently at that level. Those who stay prefer the managed version, which is comfortable and quietly disappointing for everybody involved in it. Running beneath is a conviction that the unedited version of you would clear the room within minutes. What it costs is exactly the closeness you most want, held one small distance away for years by things that nobody ever said aloud.",
    "invitation": "What have you left unsaid to somebody close to you? Say one true and slightly awkward thing out loud before Friday comes. You are allowed to be seen without the edit. If the whole of you were visible tomorrow, who would move closer instead of away?"
  },
  "60": {
    "gift": "You define the edges of things, and people living inside vagueness come toward that clarity with something close to visible relief. Whoever arrives wants somebody who will state plainly what is possible and what is not, rather than being kind about it and leaving them to guess at it. Most people blur a limit to avoid disappointing anybody, while you name it, and the person opposite you finally has something solid and real to plan around. The respect this earns lasts, because nobody who has ever dealt with you has had to guess where they stood with you.",
    "shadow": "Limitation held as a worldview pulls in people who need their own smallness confirmed by somebody else. Disappointment is nearly always the trigger — a plan collapses, the boundaries harden, and soon the only company fitting inside them are people who also stopped expecting much. What forms is a mutual agreement not to hope, which passes for hard realism among every single person taking part. Beneath it sits the conviction that wanting more is simply a setup for humiliation later. The expansive people, the ones who would have pulled you outward into something bigger, find the walls too high and eventually stop knocking on them.",
    "invitation": "Who left your life because there was no room in it to grow? Say yes this week to something slightly larger than your usual terms. You are allowed to be approached by people with big plans. What kind of justice becomes possible once your limits are serving you instead of the reverse?"
  },
  "61": {
    "gift": "You astonish people occasionally, and whoever witnesses it never entirely recovers from the encounter afterwards. Those drawn toward you are searching for something that they have no name for at all, and they recognise that you have been in contact with it. Most people discuss the deepest questions secondhand, while you speak from direct experience, and the difference is unmistakable to anybody who has been near the real thing. What gathers around you is a small number of people willing to sit with enormous questions, which is company that almost nobody else on earth gets to keep.",
    "shadow": "Untethered, the field draws people who want a prophet, and the role is offered so warmly that refusing it becomes genuinely difficult. Isolation combined with certainty is the trigger — alone with a large idea too long, and the followers turn up before the idea has been tested against anything. Those arriving need you to remain certain, so the one person capable of questioning you gets quietly edged out of the circle. Beneath it lies the assumption that ordinary company could never understand and is therefore not worth the trouble of keeping. You end up surrounded by believers with nobody left who is able to say plainly that you are wrong.",
    "invitation": "Which of the people near you is permitted to contradict you? Take one enormous idea to somebody entirely practical before this week ends. You are allowed to be ordinary company as well as extraordinary. When the followers have all gone home, who is still sitting there in the room with you?"
  },
  "62": {
    "gift": "You name things exactly, and people bring you their confusion specifically so that they will hear it finally put into plain words. Whoever comes to you has been circling one feeling for months, and they need a single accurate sentence to make the whole thing manageable again. Most people approximate and hope it lands somewhere near, while you locate the precise word, which others experience as being understood rather than analysed. The gratitude that produces is disproportionate and long-lasting, because a thing named correctly stops haunting the person who had been carrying it around for so long.",
    "shadow": "Correctness as a reflex draws people who want an argument and repels almost everybody else in the vicinity. Feeling unsure of yourself is the trigger — uncertain, you get more exact, and precision turns into a method of winning rather than a way of helping anyone. The debaters stay; anybody who came wanting comfort learns fast to take their difficult evening somewhere considerably softer. A belief sits under that: that being right is what keeps you in the room and welcome there. People begin choosing their words carefully around you, which is the exact opposite of intimacy, and the conversations grow thinner every year.",
    "invitation": "Who currently edits themselves before they speak a word to you? Let one inaccurate sentence go by this week uncorrected. You are allowed to be valued for warmth rather than accuracy. What kind of conversation opens up once being right has stopped being the point of it?"
  },
  "63": {
    "gift": "You question things properly, and people bring you the doubts they have not dared examine anywhere else in their lives. Whoever arrives is stuck inside a belief that they suspect is wrong, and they want somebody unafraid to look straight at the thing with them. Most people ask questions to confirm what they already think, while you ask in order to find out, which is obvious almost immediately to anybody paying close attention. The trust you attract comes from the fact that nobody has ever left one of your questions feeling smaller than when they first arrived.",
    "shadow": "Doubt turned inward pulls in people who are delighted to supply the certainty you have stopped supplying for yourself. They arrive with answers, take over the deciding, and the arrangement is comfortable for a while and corrosive for much longer than that. Fatigue is the trigger — worn down, every question becomes about your own worth rather than about the world in front of you. Beneath it runs the belief that until you are sure of yourself, no one could reasonably choose you. It costs your own judgement, outsourced slowly, and leaves a circle of confident people who would find you far less appealing if you started deciding things again.",
    "invitation": "Whose certainty have you been borrowing lately? Decide one thing this week without consulting a single other person. You are allowed to be trusted for your questions rather than doubted for them. If you were sure of yourself, what kind of person would find you good company?"
  },
  "64": {
    "gift": "You imagine in detail what has not happened yet, and people come toward you at the point where their own picture of the future goes blank. Those arriving are mid-transition and unable to see what comes next, and something about your company makes the possibilities become visible to them once again. Most people require the way forward drawn clearly before they will move at all, while you are comfortable in the fog, and others find that steadying at exactly the right moment. People tell you, years afterwards, that you helped them see something they had no chance whatsoever of seeing on their own.",
    "shadow": "Confusion carried too long attracts people who add to it, arriving with opinions and plans that suit them rather than you. Trying to resolve the mental static before its time is the trigger, and it sends you looking for anybody holding a confident answer. Those who respond are rarely the people who know you well, and their clarity is purchased with your own. There is an idea in there that being unresolved is unattractive, and that nobody would stay through the murky stretch. You end up with a life full of advisers, very few companions, and decisions that were never actually yours to begin with.",
    "invitation": "Whose plan for you are you currently living inside? Sit with one unresolved question this week without asking anybody about it. You are allowed to be good company while nothing is clear. When the picture forms by itself, who has stayed nearby long enough to watch it happen?"
  }
});
